<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry of Livonia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1188–after 1259</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> was a priest who took part in the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> in the eastern Baltic and wrote the Livonian Chronicle, covering events to 1227. It is the main source for the conquest and conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>. Many small Baltic principalities and Estonian counties are known almost only from his account.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia.jpg" alt="Henry of Livonia" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, copy of Henry of Livonia manuscript · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henriku_kroonika_papp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
